1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,787
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,787 --> 00:00:06,296
This is chapter three: the toolchain.

3
00:00:06,746 --> 00:00:14,982
Every JDK ships a set of tools most developers never touch, because Maven, Gradle and the IDE stand in front of them.

4
00:00:14,982 --> 00:00:20,613
But some questions only those tools can answer, and they answer them in seconds.

5
00:00:21,513 --> 00:00:22,726
Three situations.

6
00:00:22,726 --> 00:00:34,569
A dependency behaves oddly in production, and you need to know which version is really loaded, and whether it's a multi-release JAR resolving differently on this JDK.

7
00:00:35,019 --> 00:00:37,865
A colleague asks what a lambda compiles to.

8
00:00:37,865 --> 00:00:42,763
You believe it isn't an anonymous class, but you've never actually looked.

9
00:00:43,213 --> 00:00:53,177
Or you want to test a one-line idea about Collectors dot teeing, and the options seem to be a scratch project or a test nobody will keep.

10
00:00:53,177 --> 00:00:57,250
Each of these has a JDK tool that answers it in seconds.

11
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:01,144
The tools fall into five groups.

12
00:01:01,144 --> 00:01:04,045
Build tools like javac and jar.

13
00:01:04,045 --> 00:01:06,572
Run tools: java and jshell.

14
00:01:06,572 --> 00:01:10,221
Inspection: javap, jdeps and jdeprscan.

15
00:01:10,221 --> 00:01:12,654
Diagnostics: jcmd and jfr.

16
00:01:12,654 --> 00:01:15,835
And packaging: jlink and jpackage.

17
00:01:16,285 --> 00:01:19,869
Build and run, you already use through your build tool.

18
00:01:19,869 --> 00:01:27,037
Inspect and diagnose are the groups worth learning on purpose, because they answer questions nothing else can.

19
00:01:27,937 --> 00:01:30,319
jshell is the REPL, since Java 9.

20
00:01:30,319 --> 00:01:36,599
Define a list, try teeing, and see the answer immediately, without a project or a test.

21
00:01:37,049 --> 00:01:42,053
The command that makes it a real tool is slash env with a class path.

22
00:01:42,053 --> 00:01:48,654
Load your application's JAR, and you can interrogate your actual classes, not toy examples.

23
00:01:49,554 --> 00:01:51,501
javap disassembles a class.

24
00:01:51,501 --> 00:01:53,159
Take a one-line lambda.

25
00:01:53,609 --> 00:01:56,492
javap minus c minus p shows the truth.

26
00:01:56,492 --> 00:02:04,229
The body became a private static method, and the call site is an invokedynamic instruction, not a new.

27
00:02:04,229 --> 00:02:07,263
No inner class file is generated at all.

28
00:02:07,713 --> 00:02:11,628
It also settles arguments about string concatenation.

29
00:02:11,628 --> 00:02:14,878
Java 8 compiled it to a StringBuilder chain.

30
00:02:14,878 --> 00:02:21,230
Since Java 9 it's a single invokedynamic, built into an optimised strategy at runtime.

31
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:26,401
So always use StringBuilder is outdated advice for simple expressions.

32
00:02:27,301 --> 00:02:31,347
Since Java 11, java can run a source file directly.

33
00:02:31,347 --> 00:02:36,505
With Java 25's compact source files, there's no class to declare.

34
00:02:36,505 --> 00:02:40,869
Just a main method, reading a CSV and printing a count.

35
00:02:41,319 --> 00:02:44,142
Run it with java Reconcile dot java.

36
00:02:44,142 --> 00:02:48,299
It compiles in memory, runs, and leaves no artifacts.

37
00:02:48,299 --> 00:02:54,494
Java 22 added multiple files, and a shebang line makes it an executable script.

38
00:02:54,494 --> 00:03:00,455
For operational scripts, that replaces a throwaway Maven module, with types.

39
00:03:01,355 --> 00:03:05,313
jdeps is the cheapest first step of any migration.

40
00:03:05,313 --> 00:03:13,072
Minus jdkinternals lists every internal API your JAR touches, and names the supported replacement.

41
00:03:13,072 --> 00:03:19,168
Print module deps tells you which JDK modules the application actually needs.

42
00:03:19,618 --> 00:03:25,491
Feed that list to jlink, and you get a runtime containing only those modules.

43
00:03:25,491 --> 00:03:30,221
A two-hundred-megabyte JDK typically becomes about forty-five.

44
00:03:31,121 --> 00:03:34,296
Four compiler and launcher settings matter.

45
00:03:34,296 --> 00:03:38,358
Minus parameters keeps parameter names in the bytecode.

46
00:03:38,358 --> 00:03:44,561
Without it, JSON binding, dependency injection and validation see arg zero, arg one.

47
00:03:45,011 --> 00:03:48,489
Enable lint for removal and unchecked at minimum.

48
00:03:48,489 --> 00:03:53,245
Those two categories have the best ratio of real findings to noise.

49
00:03:53,695 --> 00:03:57,255
Declare annotation processor paths explicitly.

50
00:03:57,255 --> 00:04:07,626
Java 21 warns about implicit processing, and later releases turn it off, so Lombok and MapStruct builds suddenly stop generating code.

51
00:04:08,076 --> 00:04:13,793
And put JVM options in an argument file, so they're reviewable in version control.

52
00:04:14,243 --> 00:04:15,323
One last trap.

53
00:04:15,323 --> 00:04:19,566
If a flag seems ignored, check underscore JAVA OPTIONS.

54
00:04:19,566 --> 00:04:22,575
It silently overrides the command line.

55
00:04:22,575 --> 00:04:27,513
Use JAVA TOOL OPTIONS instead, which appears in the startup log.

56
00:04:28,413 --> 00:04:35,000
Multi-release JARs let a library ship different versions of a class for different JDKs.

57
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:41,511
The JVM picks the highest versioned directory that doesn't exceed the running release.

58
00:04:41,961 --> 00:04:48,097
The consequence for debugging: the class you're looking at may not be the one that runs.

59
00:04:48,097 --> 00:04:53,396
List the JAR with jar t f first, then disassemble the right versioned entry.

60
00:04:54,296 --> 00:04:55,661
Production rules.

61
00:04:55,661 --> 00:04:56,624
Learn javap.

62
00:04:56,624 --> 00:05:01,119
Run jdeps minus jdkinternals before every major upgrade.

63
00:05:01,119 --> 00:05:04,250
Enable minus parameters in every build.

64
00:05:04,250 --> 00:05:07,943
Declare annotation processor paths explicitly.

65
00:05:07,943 --> 00:05:10,913
Keep JVM options in an argument file.

66
00:05:10,913 --> 00:05:14,605
And use source launch for operational scripts.

67
00:05:15,055 --> 00:05:19,357
Never assume the IDE compiles the same way as the build.

68
00:05:19,357 --> 00:05:22,814
Never rely on implicit annotation processing.

69
00:05:22,814 --> 00:05:25,425
Never set underscore JAVA OPTIONS.

70
00:05:25,425 --> 00:05:29,804
Never debug a JAR without checking its versioned entries.

71
00:05:29,804 --> 00:05:36,026
And never ship without debug info, or you lose line numbers in every stack trace.

72
00:05:36,926 --> 00:05:37,564
To recap.

73
00:05:37,564 --> 00:05:41,320
jshell with your own classpath for quick experiments.

74
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:44,651
javap to see what the compiler really produced.

75
00:05:44,651 --> 00:05:49,046
jdeps for an upgrade work list, and jlink for a small runtime.

76
00:05:49,046 --> 00:05:54,078
And in the build: parameters, lint, and explicit annotation processors.

77
00:05:54,528 --> 00:05:58,900
Next is chapter four: builds, release flags and preview features.

78
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:00,245
Thanks for watching.
